;;; `std.bijection/bijective-setup` — the fan-out **with the blind enumerator
;;; left on**, which is what every other program in this directory turns off.
;;;
;;; ── Why this file exists ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; M1d S1d.2.4 gave `bijective-setup` two more activators to derive —
;;; `(total-owed R isa)` and `(surjective-owed R isa)` — and an activator fact
;;; is a **stored** fact. The blind enumerator builds candidates from every
;;; relation carrying a `(relation …)` signature, so the question "could a run
;;; start guessing about the obligation activators themselves" is a real one,
;;; and the answer must not be an argument about signatures. It is this file:
;;; the enumerator runs unscoped, and `ein-infer/tests/obligation_reports.rs`
;;; asserts that no `hyp` event it emits names `total-owed`, `surjective-owed`,
;;; `slot-owed-room` or `slot-owed-fill`.
;;;
;;; The six activators that were already there — `total`, `surjective`,
;;; `domain-elimination`, `range-elimination`, `functional-negative`,
;;; `injective-negative` — are in exactly the same position and were never
;;; checked either. They are covered here too, for free.
;;;
;;; ── The puzzle ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two guests, two chairs, one seating stated. The bijection machinery closes
;;; it without a single hypothesis: `functional-negative` and
;;; `injective-negative` write the two negatives Ann's chair implies, and
;;; `domain-elimination` then forces Bob into the chair that is left. So the
;;; verdict is a `Solution` at root — and the enumerator still ran, because
;;; `complete()` is what asks it whether anything is left to guess.
;;;
;;; That is the shape this check needs: a program where the enumerator is
;;; *consulted* about a KB that holds obligation activators, rather than one
;;; where a search actually happens. A search would work too and cost more.
;;;
;;; ── Why there is a second obligation, and it is not decoration ─────────────
;;;
;;; M1d S1d.2.5 made hypothesis generation a **ladder**: the puzzle's hrules if
;;; it has any, else the undischarged obligations, else the blind enumerator.
;;; A program that declares `(bijective seats)` therefore no longer reaches the
;;; blind rung at all — `total-owed` / `surjective-owed` are the generator now,
;;; and here they owe nothing, so nothing is proposed and this file's whole
;;; check went vacuous. The claim needs the enumerator, so the file has to earn
;;; it back.
;;;
;;; `sorted-somewhere` is what earns it. It is an obligation on `is-a` — every
;;; Guest must be a member of some declared sort — and `is-a` is exactly the
;;; relation `(total-owed seats is-a)`'s guard *scans*. The domain contract's
;;; C4 says a branch is jointly exhaustive only while its candidate set cannot
;;; grow underneath it, so an obligation whose scan relation the rung itself
;;; proposes is **declined**, and a declined obligation takes the whole call
;;; back to the blind enumerator. That is this file's rung: `declined`, by
;;; construction, in one `rung` event per generation call.
;;;
;;; The obligation is *satisfied* — `(is-a Ann Guest)` witnesses it through
;;; `(sort Guest Sortal)` — because the decline is static, per activator, and
;;; must not depend on the KB happening to owe something. A fixture that owed
;;; would be testing the tally instead.
;;;
;;; ── What `:no-hypothesis` names, and what it deliberately does not ─────────
;;;
;;; The two hierarchy relations and `sort`, and nothing else. A type hierarchy
;;; is not a thing to guess about — left in, the enumerator proposes
;;; `(is-a Ann Bob)` and 1.1 million of its relatives, and the file costs 22 s
;;; instead of milliseconds; `sort` is the same kind of relation and is scoped
;;; out for the same reason, which is also what keeps this file's counters
;;; where they were before the second obligation arrived.
;;; Everything else stays in scope, and that is the point:
;;; **the obligation activators are not named here**, so if the enumerator
;;; could propose `(total-owed seats is-a)` it would, and the assertion would
;;; catch it. A file that scoped them out would be testing its own
;;; `:no-hypothesis` line.
;;;
;;; `typecheck-setup` is imported for the same reason and pays for itself: with
;;; the enumerator loose on `seats`, the kernel imposes no type system
;;; (S1.7.23) and nothing else would refuse `(seats C1 Ann)` — a chair sitting
;;; on a guest. The puzzle's own typecheck is what rejects it, which is the
;;; division of labour the whole is-a-free design rests on.
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation observes ──────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Naming `seats` closes it, so the claim is that the extension is exactly the
;;; two arrows and the two negatives — which is also the claim that
;;; `domain-elimination` fired rather than a guess landing.

(import std.algebra   :symbols (includes))
(import std.bijection :symbols (bijective-properties bijective-setup typecheck-setup))

(relation is-a  T T           :why "{?1} is a {?2}")
(relation is-a* T T           :why "{?1} is a {?2} (transitively)")
(relation seats Guest Chair   :why "{?1} sits on {?2}")
(relation sort  T T           :why "{?1} is a declared sort of {?2}")

(is-a Ann Guest) (is-a Bob Guest)
(is-a C1 Chair)  (is-a C2 Chair)

(sort Guest Sortal) (sort Chair Sortal)

;; The obligation that declines the rung — see the header. It owes `is-a`, and
;; `is-a` is what `(total-owed seats is-a)`'s guard scans, so no obligation
;; here may be branched on and the blind enumerator runs.
(rule sorted-somewhere ()
  :match  (and (is-a ?x Guest)
               (absent (and (sort ?k Sortal) (is-a ?x ?k))))
  :assert (open is-a)
  :why    "{?x} belongs to no declared sort."
  :priority 500)

;; The typecheck quantifies over the transitive hierarchy; there is no
;; inheritance here, so it is `is-a` copied across.
(includes is-a is-a*)

(bijection-hierarchy is-a)
(typecheck-hierarchy is-a*)
(bijective seats)

(seats Ann C1)

(query
  :goal (seats ?who ?where)
  :no-hypothesis (is-a is-a* sort)
  :expect (model (seats Ann C1) (seats Bob C2)
                 (not (seats Ann C2)) (not (seats Bob C1))))
